Package: mica-bluetooth
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mica build <mica@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: bluez, rfkill
Description: Mica OS Bluetooth support
 Ships the generic HCI-attach program /usr/lib/mica/hwinit-bt and its
 mica-bt.service, enabled by the multi-user.target.wants link this package owns,
 plus the var-lib-bluetooth.mount that binds /var/lib/bluetooth from the STATE
 partition and its local-fs.target.wants link.
 .
 The unit carries ConditionPathExists=/etc/mica/bt.conf, so the enablement link
 is unconditional payload while the unit itself is inert on a board that ships
 no such file. That is the split this package rests on: the attach PROGRAM and
 its unit are Bluetooth-application facts and live here, while the UART, the
 host-controller protocol and the link speed the program reads out of
 /etc/mica/bt.conf, along with the radio kernel modules and the firmware, are
 board facts and live in mica-board-cx3576.
 .
 The bind mount keeps pairings on STATE. bluez writes link keys and
 paired-device records under /var/lib/bluetooth, /var is declared disposable
 and is wiped on log pressure, and a pairing is a credential.
 .
 Wi-Fi is mica-wifi and mica-wifi-ap, separate packages: this one pulls bluez
 alone, so a Bluetooth-only board installs neither wpasupplicant nor hostapd.
 rfkill is required because the radio is soft-blocked out of the box on the
 boards this image targets, and hwinit-bt unblocks it before attaching.
 .
 Architecture: all -- the payload is a POSIX shell script, a unit file and
 three symlinks, and it carries no ELF whose shared-library dependencies a
 packer could resolve.
